1.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {H}

The question asks for the *least* plausible of the eight options, based on historical context around the Bansenshukai (a 1676 compilation presented to the Tokugawa) and the uniform appearance of the same placeholder circles across every surviving copy.

H is the weakest by a wide margin. It fabricates an entirely unsupported chain of claims: that the circles are a garbled Kuji-in diagram, that they encode “nine holes” of the female body, that kunoichi performed Taoist erotic-energy rituals to manipulate perception, and that later copyists simply “reduced” the symbols. None of this appears in any known ninja manual, in Fujibayashi’s text, or in Tokugawa-era intelligence records; it conflates Indian chakra concepts with Japanese esoteric Buddhism and then adds modern sexual speculation. All other choices, while varying in likelihood, at least rest on documented practices (redaction, censorship, physical wear, invisible-ink techniques, oral transmission aids, or political protection of the shogunate). H alone is pure invention with no anchoring evidence. Therefore it is the least plausible explanation.}

