2.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleague 1 makes a strong case that H is pure modern invention with no grounding in any surviving ninja manuals or Tokugawa records; the leap from Kuji-in hand seals to “nine holes” and erotic-energy rituals simply has no textual or historical anchor. At the same time, Colleague 2’s observation about the uniform blanks across every independent copy does weaken G, because random wear would almost certainly produce irregular gaps rather than identical placeholders.  

On C, the 1676 compilation date versus Lady Saigō’s 1589 death is a real chronological gap, yet it may not be fatal. Fujibayashi’s work drew on earlier Iga and Kōga traditions that predated the Edo period; if those traditions already encoded politically sensitive kunoichi methods linked to the Tokugawa founding, later copyists could still have felt pressure to redact them even a century afterward. The concern would have been less about Saigō’s personal actions and more about preserving the sanitized origin story of the shogunate. That keeps C within the realm of plausible (if speculative) political caution, whereas H remains untethered from any documented practice.  

This leaves us weighing the remaining options more carefully—particularly whether deliberate institutional redaction (B or D) or a concealment technique (E or F) better explains the consistent blanks.}

